  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe approach combines multi-stage grid refinement with penalty-constrained optimization to minimize the maximum overlap integral. Starting from a base 45-point grid, we systematically double the resolution (to 90, 180, and potentially 360 points) at each stage. At each stage, we interpolate the previous solution, emphasize integral constraints with penalties, and use L-BFGS-B optimization to minimize the maximized overlap. The penalty gradient strategy ensures active constraint handling, while Jacobian-based integral correction precisely maintains feasibility. This method directly targets the original objective while allowing progressive resolution improvement.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nWe represent h(x) as a piecewise-constant function on [0,2] with n = 45 * 2^k points (k=0,1,2,3). The grid is defined by dx = 2/n. The overlap is computed by correlation:\noverlap\u2096 = dx * max(correlation coefficient)\nConstraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx =1 \u21e8 sum(h) = 180 * 2^k/2\nPenalty: f(h) = max\u2096(overlap\u2096) + \u03c1 * (1/self.sum(h) -1)**(-2)\nwhere \u03c1 scales exponentially with stage (1e6, 1e9, 1e12)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Validate initial_h_values (45 points): compute sum, enforce bounds, calculate initial overlap\n\n2. Stage-based refinement:\n   - For each stage in [1,3] (total grid points: 45,90,180):\n     1. Interpolate from base solution (linear interpolation)\n     2. Set penalty \u03c1 = [1e6,1e9,1e12][stage-1]\n     3. Define Jacobian-vector product for integral constraints\n     4. Optimize using L-BFGS-B:\n          obj = overlap + \u03c1 * (1/sum(h) -1)**(-2)\n          bounds: [0,1] for all variables\n     5. Apply Hessian-informed Lehmer weighting to penalty\n     6. Sample resolutions using factorial interpolation points\n\n3. Integral maintenance:\n   - Use log-transformed variable space for sum constraints\n   - Jacobian = d(sum(h))/(dx d(h_j)) = [1,1,1,...]\n\n4. Termination criteria:\n   - Stop at +3 stages if time exhausted\n   - Break if overlapping improvement <1e-4\n   - Preserve best solution at each stage\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n\n- Penalties: \u03c1 scales by 3^{stage} to enforce constraints\n- Dichotomy interpolation: favors sharp transitions\n- Bi-level sampling: stage1=45, stage2=90, stage3=180\n- Flexible grid: defines grid points uniformly\n- Integral recovery: self.lehmer_weighting\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n\n- Stage45: 1000 O(n\u00b2) evaluations\n- Stage90: 1000 O(n\u00b2) (n=90) \u2248 270 million operations\n- Stage360: 2^7 * O(n\u00b2) would exceed budget\n\nFallback strategy:\n\n- If optimization fails: repair solution by gradient projection\n- If Jacobian unavailable: revert to quadratic projection\n- Safety net: keep best solution from previous stage\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n- Compute pairwise overlap by correlation\n- Validate: 0\u2264h\u22641, sum(h)\u2208[n/2-0.5, n/2+0.5]\n- Score = dx * max(lagged correlations)\n- Conditional fallback: return initial_h_values if integral error >0.01\n- Precision: dx=2.0/n_points",
